7. Serialization Profiles

Atlas recognizes multiple serialization profiles.

Examples include:

Profile	Intended Use
JSON	Web communication
XML	Enterprise integration
YAML	Human authoring
Protocol Buffers	High-performance communication
CBOR	Compact binary exchange
RDF	Semantic interoperability
Future Profiles	Additional representations

Profiles SHALL remain implementation-independent.

8. Serialized Object Structure

Every serialized Atlas object SHALL include:

Property	Description
Object Identifier	Persistent identity
Canonical Type	Object classification
Schema Version	Serialization schema
Object Version	Version reference
Metadata	Canonical metadata
Payload	Object content
Relationships	Referenced objects
Validation Information	Validation references

Representations MAY omit derived information if reconstruction remains possible.

9. Schema Evolution

Serialized representations SHALL support:

Schema version identification
Backward compatibility
Forward compatibility
Deprecation notices
Migration rules
Extension mechanisms

Schema evolution SHALL preserve semantic compatibility whenever practical.

10. Serialization Validation

Before exchange or persistence, serialized objects SHALL be validated for:

Structural correctness
Schema conformance
Required properties
Identifier integrity
Version compatibility
Metadata completeness

Validation SHALL precede deserialization where practical.

11. Interoperability

Atlas serialization SHALL enable exchange between independent implementations.

Implementations SHALL preserve:

Identity
Metadata
Governance
Provenance
Relationships
Lifecycle state

Encoding differences SHALL NOT affect interoperability.

12. Archival

Serialized representations intended for archival SHALL preserve:

Canonical identity
Version information
Metadata
Provenance
Relationships
Validation history

Archived representations SHALL remain recoverable.

13. Security

Serialization SHALL preserve security information including:

Security classification
Integrity verification references
Digital signature references
Encryption metadata
Trust references

Security metadata SHALL remain independent of encoding format.

14. Governance

Every serialization profile SHALL define:

Supported schema versions
Validation rules
Compatibility guarantees
Deprecation policy
Extension policy
Audit requirements

Governance SHALL accompany the serialization profile.

15. Quality Requirements

Every serialized representation SHALL satisfy:

Completeness
Consistency
Integrity
Recoverability
Interoperability
Determinism
Version compatibility
Long-term readability

Quality SHALL be measurable.

16. Conformance

A serialization implementation conforms if it:

Preserves canonical semantics.
Supports reversible serialization.
Preserves identifiers.
Preserves metadata.
Supports schema evolution.
Maintains interoperability.
Implements serialization governance.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Canonical binary profiles.
Semantic compression.
Streaming serialization.
Zero-copy representations.
Distributed object serialization.
Digital Twin serialization profiles.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical serialization semantics.

